 ## Copies Need a Depth Policy
 
+
+
+<!-- cell core19_copy_depth_graph -->
+The same outer-copy policy can produce different observations depending on which inner objects are shared. Read the following as an object diagram, not runnable C#:
+
+```text
+orderA -> [lines A] -> [line A] --product--> [product P: price 10]
+orderB -> [lines B] -> [line B] --product---^
+```
+
+The two orders own different line collections and line objects, but their lines refer to the same product P. Changing the quantity on line A affects only order A. If P were mutable, changing P's price would be visible through both orders. If P is immutable, a price change instead requires a new product object and a deliberate replacement policy.
+
+| Action, considered independently | Order A observes | Order B observes | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Add a line to collection A | Another line | Same line count | Outer collections differ. |
+| Change quantity on line A | New quantity | Same quantity on line B | Line objects differ. |
+| Change shared mutable P's price to 12 | Price 12 | Price 12 | Both product references reach P. |
+| Shallow-copy order A while retaining its line-collection reference, then add through the copy | Another line | Same line count | The copy and A share collection A, not collection B. |
+
+**Predict before revealing:** does copying only collection A also duplicate line A and product P?
+
+```{dropdown} Follow the references
+No. A new outer collection can still contain a reference to the same line A, which in turn still refers to P. Copying the collection, copying each line, and copying products are three different decisions. Name the mutations that must be independent, then copy the objects needed to enforce that policy. Sharing an immutable product can be intentional.
+```
+
+The diagram makes the later composition rule concrete: owning a collection is not automatically owning independent copies of every object reachable from its elements.
 
 
